    "prompt": "Please comment briefly on the following solution.\nQuestion: \"There exists a polynomial $P$ of degree 5 with the following property: If $z$ is a complex number such that $z^5 + 2004z = 1,$ then $P(z^2) = 0.$  Calculate\n\\[\\frac{P(1)}{P(-1)}.\\]\"\nSolution: \"Let $r_1,$ $r_2,$ $r_3,$ $r_4,$ $r_5$ be the roots of $Q(z) = z^5 + 2004z - 1.$  Then\n\\[Q(z) = (z - r_1)(z - r_2)(z - r_3)(z - r_4)(z - r_5)\\]and\n\\[P(z) = c(z - r_1^2)(z - r_2^2)(z - r_3^2)(z - r_4^2)(z - r_5^2)\\]for some constant $c.$\n\nHence,\n\\begin{align*}\n\\frac{P(1)}{P(-1)} &= \\frac{c(1 - r_1^2)(1 - r_2^2)(1 - r_3^2)(1 - r_4^2)(1 - r_5^2)}{c(-1 - r_1^2)(-1 - r_2^2)(-1 - r_3^2)(-1 - r_4^2)(-1 - r_5^2)} \\\\\n&= -\\frac{(1 - r_1^2)(1 - r_2^2)(1 - r_3^2)(1 - r_4^2)(1 - r_5^2)}{(1 + r_1^2)(1 + r_2^2)(1 + r_3^2)(1 + r_4^2)(1 + r_5^2)} \\\\\n&= -\\frac{(1 - r_1)(1 - r_2)(1 - r_3)(1 - r_4)(1 - r_5)(1 + r_1)(1 + r_2)(1 + r_3)(1 + r_4)(1 + r_5)}{(i + r_1)(i + r_2)(i + r_3)(i + r_4)(i + r_5)(-i + r_1)(-i + r_2)(-i + r_3)(-i + r_4)(-i + r_5)} \\\\\n&= \\frac{(1 - r_1)(1 - r_2)(1 - r_3)(1 - r_4)(1 - r_5)(-1 - r_1)(-1 - r_2)(-1 - r_3)(-1 - r_4)(-1 - r_5)}{(-i - r_1)(-i - r_2)(-i - r_3)(-i - r_4)(-i - r_5)(-i - r_1)(i - r_2)(i - r_3)(i - r_4)(i - r_5)} \\\\\n&= \\frac{Q(1) Q(-1)}{Q(i) Q(-i)} \\\\\n&= \\frac{(1 + 2004 - 1)(-1 - 2004 - 1)}{(i^5 + 2004i - 1)((-i)^5 - 2004i - 1)} \\\\\n&= \\frac{(2004)(-2006)}{(-1 + 2005i)(-1 - 2005i))} \\\\\n&= \\frac{(2004)(-2006)}{1^2 + 2005^2} \\\\\n&= \\boxed{-\\frac{2010012}{2010013}}.\n\\end{align*}\"",
    "condition": "neutral"
